`,e.jsxs(a.p,{children:["The ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"hbase.rsgroup.grouploadbalancer.class"})," config has been deprecated, as now the top level load balancer will always be ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"RSGroupBasedLoadBalaner"}),", and the ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"hbase.master.loadbalancer.class"})," config is for configuring the balancer within a group. This also means you should not set ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"hbase.master.loadbalancer.class"})," to ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"RSGroupBasedLoadBalaner"})," any more even if rsgroup feature is enabled."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(a.p,{children:["And we have done some special changes for compatibility. First, if coprocessor ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"org.apache.hadoop.hbase.rsgroup.RSGroupAdminEndpoint"})," is specified, the ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"hbase.balancer.rsgroup.enabled"})," flag will be set to true automatically to enable rs group feature. Second, we will load ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"hbase.rsgroup.grouploadbalancer.class"})," prior to ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"hbase.master.loadbalancer.class"}),". And last, if you do not set ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"hbase.rsgroup.grouploadbalancer.class"})," but only set ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"hbase.master.loadbalancer.class"})," to ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"RSGroupBasedLoadBalancer"}),", we will load the default load balancer to avoid infinite nesting. This means you do not need to change anything when upgrading if you have already enabled rs group feature."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(a.p,{children:["The main difference comparing to the old implementation is that, now the rsgroup for a table is stored in ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"TableDescriptor"}),", instead of in ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"RSGroupInfo"}),", so the ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"getTables"})," method of ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"RSGroupInfo"})," has been deprecated. And if you use the ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"Admin"})," methods to get the ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"RSGroupInfo"}),", its ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"getTables"})," method will always return empty. This is because that in the old implementation, this method is a bit broken as you can set rsgroup on namespace and make all the tables under this namespace into this group but you can not get these tables through ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"RSGroupInfo.getTables"}),". Now you should use the two new methods ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"listTablesInRSGroup"})," and ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"getConfiguredNamespacesAndTablesInRSGroup"})," in ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"Admin"})," to get tables and namespaces in a rsgroup."]}),`
`,e.jsx(a.p,{children:"Of course the behavior for the old RSGroupAdminEndpoint is not changed, we will fill the tables field of the RSGroupInfo before returning, to make it compatible with old hbase client/shell."}),`
`,e.jsxs(a.p,{children:["When upgrading, the migration between the RSGroupInfo and TableDescriptor will be done automatically. It will take sometime, but it is fine to restart master in the middle, the migration will continue after restart. And during the migration, the rs group feature will still work and in most cases the region will not be misplaced(since this is only a one time job and will not last too long so we have not test it very seriously to make sure the region will not be misplaced always, so we use the word 'in most cases'). The implementation is a bit tricky, you can see the code in ",e.jsx(a.code,{children:"RSGroupInfoManagerImpl.migrate"})," if interested."]}),`
`,e.jsx(a.h2,{id:"region-normalizer",children:"Region Normalizer"}),`
`,e.jsx(a.p,{children:'The Region Normalizer tries to make Regions all in a table about the same in size. It does this by first calculating total table size and average size per region. It splits any region that is larger than twice this size. Any region that is much smaller is merged into an adjacent region. The Normalizer runs on a regular schedule, which is configurable. It can also be disabled entirely via a runtime "switch". It can be run manually via the shell or Admin API call. Even if normally disabled, it is good to run manually after the cluster has been running a while or say after a burst of activity such as a large delete.'}),`
`,e.jsx(a.p,{children:"The Normalizer works well for bringing a table's region boundaries into alignment with the reality of data distribution after an initial effort at pre-splitting a table. It is also a nice compliment to the data TTL feature when the schema includes timestamp in the rowkey, as it will automatically merge away regions whose contents have expired."}),`
`,e.jsxs(a.p,{children:["(The bulk of the below detail was copied wholesale from the blog by Romil Choksi at ",e.jsx(a.a,{href:"https://community.cloudera.com/t5/Community-Articles/HBase-Region-Normalizer/ta-p/247266",children:"HBase Region Normalizer"}),")."]}),`
`,e.jsx(a.p,{children:"The Region Normalizer is feature available since HBase-1.2. It runs a set of pre-calculated merge/split actions to resize regions that are either too large or too small compared to the average region size for a given table. Region Normalizer when invoked computes a normalization 'plan' for all of the tables in HBase. System tables (such as hbase:meta, hbase:namespace, Phoenix system tables etc) and user tables with normalization disabled are ignored while computing the plan. For normalization enabled tables, normalization plan is carried out in parallel across multiple tables."}),`
`,e.jsx(a.p,{children:"Normalizer can be enabled or disabled globally for the entire cluster using the ‘normalizer_switch' command in the HBase shell. Normalization can also be controlled on a per table basis, which is disabled by default when a table is created. Normalization for a table can be enabled or disabled by setting the NORMALIZATION_ENABLED table attribute to true or false."}),`
`,e.jsx(a.p,{children:"To check normalizer status and enable/disable normalizer"}),`
